<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tughlaq dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tughlaq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tughlaq dynasty ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> from 1320 to the early fifteenth century. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a> the sultanate reached its greatest territorial extent, but his ambitious schemes, such as the transfer of the capital and the token currency, caused serious disruption. Firuz Shah Tughlaq's later reign was more stable, but the dynasty was severely weakened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-delhi-1398">sack of Delhi</a> in 1398.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty.jpg" alt="Tughlaq dynasty" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maps created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan; Territorial area: पाटलिपुत्र, per Schwartberg Atlas p.148 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Tughlaqs.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Tughlaq dynasty was the third to rule the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. Tughlaq was the personal name of the founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-tughluq">Ghazi Malik</a>, rather than an ancestral designation, and historians use it for the whole dynasty as a convenience. His ancestry is debated, but he is usually considered to be of Turko-Mongol or Turkic origin. The Khalji dynasty had ruled before 1320. Its last ruler, Khusro Khan, lacked the support of the Muslim nobles, who invited Ghazi Malik, then governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, to lead a coup. In 1320 he killed Khusro Khan and took the throne as Ghiyath al-Din Tughluq.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq built Tughlakabad, a city six kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> with a fort considered more defensible against Mongol attacks. His son Jauna Khan captured Arangal in 1323 and annexed Kakatiya territory. According to many historians, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, Ghiyasuddin was killed by Jauna Khan in 1325, and Jauna Khan ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a> for 26 years. His campaigns between 1330 and 1335 brought the sultanate to its territorial zenith, but they were expensive and the extended empire was hard to retain. Rebellions became routine, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> became independent.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughlaq">Muhammad bin Tughlaq</a> raised the land tax on non-Muslims tenfold in some districts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> region and twentyfold in others. Villages left their fields, and famines followed; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, who arrived in 1334, noted a seven-year famine from 1335 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. The court historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziauddin-barani">Ziauddin Barani</a> records bloodshed among Muslims as well. After a short rule by Mahmud Ibn Muhammad, his nephew Firuz Shah Tughlaq reigned for 37 years; Barani calls him the mildest sovereign since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> came to Delhi. Civil wars broke out in 1384 and 1394.</p><p>In 1398 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> defeated four armies of the Sultanate. Sultan Mahmud Khan fled, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> was plundered for eight days and its population massacred. Khizr Khan, whom Timur is believed to have appointed viceroy at Delhi, entered the city on 6 June 1414 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-dynasty">Sayyid dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Tughlaqs managed their expanded empire by appointing family members and Muslim aristocrats as na'ib of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iqta">iqta</a>' provinces under contract. The na'ib deposited a fixed sum with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> and kept a share of the taxes collected; excess collected from non-Muslims was split between na'ib and Sultan in a 20:80 ratio, which Firuz Shah changed to 80:20. Campaigns yielded loot and slaves, and the sultans patronised a slave market. The sultans, particularly Firuz Shah, also sponsored many construction projects and are credited with developing Indo-Islamic architecture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tughlaq dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Tughluq</b> armies failed to recover the region, and by his death in 1358 Bahman Shah controlled a kingdom stretching from the Arabian Sea coast to the borders of Telangana, bounded in the south by the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers, where it faced the newly founded Vijayanagara Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A second <b>Tughluq</b> invasion against Ilyas Shah's son Sikandar Shah in 1359 ended in a negotiated peace, and Delhi thereafter accepted Bengal's independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Five successive dynasties, the Mamluk, Khalji, <b>Tughlaq</b>, Sayyid and Lodi, governed the sultanate across 320 years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Shortly afterwards, as <b>Tughluq</b> authority in the south crumbled, the brothers Harihara I and Bukka I of the Sangama family established their own kingdom on the Tungabhadra, traditionally in 1336, with its capital at the city they named Vijayanagara, &quot;city of victory&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1394 the <b>Tughluq</b> sultan appointed the eunuch Malik Sarwar governor of the eastern provinces with the title Malik al-Sharq, lord of the east.</blockquote><a href="/state/jaunpur-sultanate">Jaunpur Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Dilawar Khan Ghuri, appointed governor of Malwa by the <b>Tughluqs</b> in 1392, ruled independently after Timur's sack of Delhi in 1398 and formally declared independence around 1401.</blockquote><a href="/state/malwa-sultanate">Malwa Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
